# # /aegis/
#
# Permanent identifiers for Aegis, an open, standards-based digital
# identity platform built by Protoworks, a one-person startup in Cape Town,
# South Africa.
#
# https://w3id.org/aegis/ redirects to https://ns.aegis.protoworks.dev/
#
# ## Identifiers
#
# https://w3id.org/aegis/didcomm/v1
#   The Aegis DIDComm JSON-LD context, version 1. It defines the DIDComm
#   service terms (did-communication, DIDCommMessaging, priority,
#   recipientKeys, routingKeys, accept, uri) used in DID documents.
#
# https://w3id.org/aegis/didcomm#<term>
#   The vocabulary those terms expand to; each term is documented at its
#   anchor on the vocabulary page.
#
# ## Contact
# This space is administered by:
#
# Richard Peters
# GitHub username: Executioner1939

RewriteEngine on

# The context, version 1. JSON-LD processors ask for application/ld+json
# or application/json and get the context document itself.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_ACCEPT} application/ld\+json [OR]
RewriteCond %{HTTP_ACCEPT} application/json
RewriteRule ^didcomm/v1$ https://ns.aegis.protoworks.dev/didcomm/v1 [R=302,L]

# A browser asking for HTML gets the page that documents the context.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_ACCEPT} text/html
RewriteRule ^didcomm/v1$ https://ns.aegis.protoworks.dev/didcomm/ [R=303,L]

# Any other client gets the context document, which is what the URL names.
RewriteRule ^didcomm/v1$ https://ns.aegis.protoworks.dev/didcomm/v1 [R=302,L]

# The vocabulary namespace. A term IRI such as
# https://w3id.org/aegis/didcomm#priority arrives here without its
# fragment; the page carries an anchor for every term.
RewriteRule ^didcomm/?$ https://ns.aegis.protoworks.dev/didcomm/ [R=303,L]

# The identifier root.
RewriteRule ^$ https://ns.aegis.protoworks.dev/ [R=302,L]
